      <skos:notation rdf:datatype="http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_3">bxx</skos:notation>
      <skos:notation rdf:datatype="http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#XML_LNG">bxx</skos:notation>
      <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Borna (Democratic Republic of Congo)</skos:prefLabel>
      <dc:identifier>BXX</dc:identifier>
      <atold:op-code>BXX</atold:op-code>
      <at:op-code>BXX</at:op-code>
      <at:start.use>1950-05-09</at:start.use>
      <at:authority-code>BXX</at:authority-code>
      <at:op-mapped-code>
         <at:MappedCode>
            <dc:source>iso-639-3</dc:source>
            <at:legacy-code>bxx</at:legacy-code>
         </at:MappedCode>
      </at:op-mapped-code>
      <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">This code is deprecated in ISO 639-3. It is no longer in active use, but still has the meaning assigned to it when it was established in the Standard.</skos:historyNote>
      <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Borna (Democratic Republic of Congo) [bxx], alternative name Eborna, is given as an unclassifed Southern Bantoid language of the DRC without a population estimate and
without a location. The entry must emanate from Welmers (1971:786) since that is the only source mentioning such a language with exactly that much (lack of) information. Welmers' list, in turn, derives from sheets of African languages at the US Office of Education started
in 1959 (Welmers 1971:761). Since these sheets were hand-written there is considerable likelihood that Borna is a misreading of Boma or eBoma [ebo].</skos:scopeNote>
      <at:end.use>2015-01-12</at:end.use>
   </skos:Concept>
